WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.720
- [Narrator] Annie Londonderry
is famous for being

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.500
the first woman to
bicycle around the world.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.310
But, there's more to the story.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:11.267
And, the story starts, as
most things do—in a dark bar.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:14.040
- There is no possible
way a woman could ride

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.790
a bicycle around the world.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.760
- Can you imagine a woman
trying to do it in a skirt?

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.473
- If a woman could do
that, I'd pay $10,000.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.500
- I, Annie Londonderry,
will take that wager.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.167
(playful music)

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.680
- [Narrator] When Annie took that wager,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.060
she was oozing with
confidence and charisma.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.430
But, here's the thing, she had
never even touched a bicycle.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.890
In the late 1800’s, bicycles were seen

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.350
as dangerous for women
and very unfeminine.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.640
Riding a bicycle was a revolutionary act.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.550
Legend has it, she cycled
from Boston to New York City

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.560
to Paris riding her bike
one-footed after an injury.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.380
To India, hunting tigers with royalty.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.930
Singapore, Hong Kong,
getting caught up in a war

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.630
and sustaining a gunshot
wound to her shoulder.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.540
Nagasaki, being interned
in a Japanese prison.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.590
How did she get over oceans?

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:08.692
Don't worry about it.

00:01:08.692 --> 00:01:09.720
(ding)

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.603
San Francisco, Los Angeles, Phoenix

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:15.730
and all the way back to
Boston in exactly 15 months.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:17.040
She did it.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.020
The End.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.660
- Well, not all of that was exactly true.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:21.660
- [Narrator] And, you are?

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.783
- I'm Peter Zheutlin, and Annie
was a my great-grandaunt.

00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:28.000
- [Narrator] OK, so then
what actually happened?

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.830
- [Peter] She was full of tall tales.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.720
Even her name, Annie
Londonderry, wasn't real.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.490
Her real name was Annie Cohen Kopchovsky.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.790
She even concocted the story of the wager.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.443
And she definitely was not shot

00:01:39.443 --> 00:01:42.680
or thrown into a prison during
the Chinese-Japanese War.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:43.950
- [Narrator] Well, what
about all that other stuff

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.250
like the leg injury?

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.540
- [Peter] She did injure her leg.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.657
- [Narrator] How about hunting
for tigers with royalty?

00:01:48.657 --> 00:01:49.810
- [Peter} That's false.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.440
- [Narrator] So, with some true stuff

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.390
and some not so true stuff,
how do you view her journey?

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.040
- Although hers was a
remarkable physical achievement,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.130
her real achievement was
becoming world-famous as a symbol

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.160
of the women's liberation
movement in the 1890s.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.490
- [Narrator] Annie's sheer
audacity and the way she fought

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.560
for herself, for her own
adventures, on her own terms,

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.340
was remarkable for her time.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.496
Even if her story was kinda, sorta,

00:02:13.496 --> 00:02:15.253
(ding)
not totally accurate.

